      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm looking at some data for a new roundabout design near a major junction in london. can you give me a quick comparison of how signalized intersections vs roundabouts typically handle traffic throughput and what the main differences are in terms of delay and flow? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Signalized intersections usually optimize traffic flow when traffic conditions are constant, often resulting in slightly quicker throughput compared to roundabouts. However, they may experience more significant delays during off-peak times when fewer vehicles are present. In contrast, roundabouts tend to reduce delays generally because the continuous flow of traffic and decreased stopping points (compared to many signal phases) lead to smoother traffic progression. Still, they might experience low throughput during periods of heavy congestion due to the assigned right-of-way rules. Therefore, roundabouts often prioritize safety and reduced delay over the maximum throughput found at signalized crossroads.",
